    4. UK Company Car Tax (Benefit-in-Kind) & Salary Sacrifice Savings
  </h2>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    Beyond fuel savings, the biggest driver of UK electric vehicle adoption is HMRC's <strong>Benefit-in-Kind (BiK) company car tax system</strong>. While petrol and diesel company cars are taxed at <strong>25% to 37%</strong> of their P11D list price depending on CO2 emissions, pure zero-emission EVs enjoy rock-bottom BiK rates:
  </p>
  <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-6 space-y-2 text-sm text-gray-800 font-medium">
    <li><strong>2025/2026 Tax Year:</strong> 3% BiK Rate</li>
    <li><strong>2026/2027 Tax Year:</strong> 4% BiK Rate</li>
    <li><strong>2027/2028 Tax Year:</strong> 5% BiK Rate</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="mb-6 leading-relaxed">
    For a UK higher-rate (40%) taxpayer choosing a £45,000 company car, switching from a 32% BiK petrol SUV (£5,760/yr in personal tax) to a 4% BiK electric SUV (£720/yr in personal tax) saves <strong>£5,040 every year in income tax alone</strong>—even before counting £1,400+ in annual petrol savings on an Octopus Go tariff.
  </p>

