  <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight">
    5. What About VED Road Tax & Terraced-House Street Charging?
  </h2>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    Since April 2025, electric vehicles in the UK are no longer exempt from <strong>Vehicle Excise Duty (VED)</strong> road tax. Most EVs pay the standard £195 annual rate (plus the Expensive Car Supplement if the list price exceeded £40,000 when registered, though recent thresholds and adjustments apply—always verify current DVLA bands for your registration year). Even factoring in standard VED, home-charged EVs remain £900 to £1,400 cheaper per year to run than petrol cars.
  </p>
  <p class="mb-8 leading-relaxed">
    <strong>No driveway?</strong> If you live in a terraced home or flat without off-street parking and rely 100% on 79p/kWh motorway rapid chargers, your fuel bill will match or slightly exceed a frugal petrol or hybrid car. However, if you have access to local council pavement gullies, workplace charging, or supermarket 22kW–50kW off-peak deals (around 35p–45p/kWh), EVs still deliver steady per-mile savings plus drastically lower servicing bills.
  </p>

